  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1201 PM CDT Sun Aug 23 2026\n\nEarlier this morning, an area of weakening convection was \nmoving across north central Kansas as an upper level disturbance\nembedded in a 10-15 knot west/northwest flow crosses western \nKansas. A 500mb high remains in place across the southwest \nUnited States while an upper level trough was located over the \nGreat Lakes Region. At the surface a surface boundary extended \nfrom eastern Colorado to the Panhandle of Texas. \n\nModels today remain in good agreement and have run to run \nconsistency. These models show minimal movement of the surface \nboundary over the next 36 hours as the next subtle upper level \ntrough rotates around the 500mb high and approaches western \nKansas late today. As this upper level system moves out into \nthe west central high plains late today there will be a chance \nfor scattered afternoon thunderstorms over eastern Colorado and \nalong the surface boundary which will be located near the \nOklahoma Panhandle border which, if they develop, will move \nsoutheast. These storms are not expected to be severe, but a few\nmay be capable of producing gusty winds. As for the possible\nthunderstorms that may develop over eastern Colorado late\ntoday...they weaken and cross extreme southwest Kansas later\ntonight. \n\nFor the first few days of this upcoming work week the 500mb \nhigh over the southwest United states will move very slowly \nwestward which will result in placing southwest Kansas on the \nnortheast edge of the upper highs and in a northwest flow \nregime. Based on this southwest Kansas can look forward to \nmultiple opportunities for scattered thunderstorms as additional\nupper waves rotate out of the Rockies and into the Plains. \n\nAs for the storms on Monday...conditions are expected to be \nsimilar to those anticipated later tonight. However, the timing \nof the upper wave crossing the Rockies appears to be earlier in \nthe day, potentially providing a better opportunity for late day\nconvection along the surface boundary near extreme southwest \nKansas. Large low level lapse rages and forecast DCAPE late day \nalso suggests a few storms may be capable of producing wind \ngusts of 50 to near 60 mph that if a few late day/evening storms\ndo develop and move into extreme southwest Kansas.\n\nA better chance for thunderstorms is expected late Tuesday as \nthe next upper level trough moves across the Western High \nPlains. Latest ensembles continue to show strong consensus on \ntiming and track of this next upper wave. This upper wave will\nbe associated with the right entrance region of an upper level \njet as it crosses the Northern Plains. Ahead of this Tuesday \nupper wave the ensembles indicate now a 50-60% probability of \nMUCAPE >500j/kg and bulk shear >30 knots over western Kansas \nalong with southeasterly upslope flow of near 70 degree \ndewpoints. This not only support another chance for \nthunderstorms across southwest Kansas late Tuesday as this upper\nwave passes but it also suggests that there is a higher \nprobability than earlier in the week that a few of these storms \nmay be severe. Locally heavy rainfall will also be possible \ngiven ensemble PWAT values near 1.5 inches (roughly 80% of \nnormal). Widespread beneficial rainfall may also be possible \nfor some areas of southwest Kansas from this Tuesday night \nevent. The latest NBM guidance has a 50 to 60% chance that \nrainfall totals Tuesday night will be greater than 0.25 inches \nwest of highway 283. \n\nThis upper level trough crossing western Kansas Tuesday night \nwill not only bring a +60% chance for thunderstorms to southwest\nKansas Tuesday night but it will also allow a cold front to \nmove into southwest Kansas on Wednesday. This cold front will \nbriefly bring an end to the hot (near 100 degree) temperatures \nacross southwest Kansas Monday and Tuesday. Highs mid week will \nbe more seasonal with highs mainly in the lower 90s. Latest NBM\neven has a 60-75% chance for highs to be less than 90 degrees \nalong the I-70 corridor and around the Garden City area.",
